⭐ Unit 5 — What You Need to Remember
Applications
Applications can contain vulnerabilities.
Never assume user input is safe.
SQL injection attacks database queries.
XSS attacks can inject malicious script into webpages.
Buffer overflows write beyond an allocated buffer.
Buffer overreads read beyond the intended buffer.
Directory traversal attempts to access files outside the intended directory.
Data
Data at rest = stored.
Data in transit = moving.
Data in use = being processed.
Sensitive data should receive stronger protection.
PII can identify an individual.
Organizations may have legal or regulatory requirements for certain data.
Access control
RBAC → permissions based on roles.
RuBAC → permissions based on rules.
DAC → resource owner controls access.
MAC → centralized security policy controls access.
Least privilege → only give the permissions necessary.
Cryptography
Encryption protects confidentiality and can be reversed with the appropriate key.
Hashing produces a one-way digest.
Salting adds random data before password hashing.
Symmetric encryption uses the same secret key for encryption and decryption.
AES is a symmetric encryption algorithm.
Asymmetric cryptography uses a public/private key pair.
RSA and ECC are examples of asymmetric cryptography.
Public keys can be shared.
Private keys must be protected.
Digital signatures can provide authentication and integrity.
Application security
Security should be considered during application design.
Secure defaults reduce risk.
Input should be validated and sanitized.
Applications should not blindly trust user input.
Detection
Logs provide evidence of activity.
Hashes can help detect file changes.
Honeypots attract or identify suspicious activity.
Honeytokens are fake resources designed to detect unauthorized use.
DLP helps identify and prevent inappropriate movement of sensitive information.
Detection systems must balance speed, accuracy, cost, and performance.
A false positive is an alert for something that is not actually malicious.
A false negative is when malicious activity is missed.
